Break tests — the site sync and the prefixes the lifecycle writes (ADR-0044)
Run on 2026-08-08 in the chat session's own clone, offline: no AWS, no cost.

Number 0 is not a planted failure. It is `scripts/publish-site.sh` exactly as it
stood at 4600aeb, the commit whose push deleted every published test result, run
against the new check. The check reddens on the real defect, which is the
strongest form this evidence takes.

Two things went wrong while producing this log, and both are kept here because
each is a documented trap in docs/session-primer.md walked into again:

  COMMIT BEFORE BREAKING THINGS ON PURPOSE. The first attempt at this sweep used
  `git checkout --` to restore each deliberate break while the FIX to
  publish-site.sh was still uncommitted. It restored the file to HEAD - which is
  the broken version - and silently discarded the fix. Same shape as the ci.yml
  pinning edit lost on 2026-07-28. Everything below was run from a committed
  tree.

  A BREAK TEST THAT FAILS TO BREAK is testing your assumption about the tool.
  Number 2 came back GREEN: the check asked whether the string "--delete" was in
  publish-site.sh, and the file's own comment explains what `aws s3 sync
  --delete` does, so that refusal could never fire under any rewrite. Fixed to
  look for it as an ARGUMENT on a non-comment line, and re-run at the end of
  this file, where it fires with the word still present in the comment.


